  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Find the intervals where the function $F(t)=f(t)\\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{t}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(-t)\\,j+{3}^{t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(t+7)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A vector function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first  and third components, $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{t}^{2}$ and ${3}^{t},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(-t)$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nSo,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0).$  \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable on $(-1,1)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>1.$\n\n- The second and third components, component $t+7$  and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nHence,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is  $(-1,1).$    \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cap (-1,1)=(-1,0).$",
